From e09bde5c4c139e73f244e355f756c857abc0846f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jose Miguel Ochoa <josemiguelo.ochoa@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 13:08:15 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: hci_event: strip Broadcom vendor bits from legacy
 adv report type

On Apple T2 Macs whose Broadcom controller runs its ROM firmware (no
patchram available, e.g. BCM4364B3 "Trinidad" on MacBookPro16,1 via
hci_uart_bcm), the LE Advertising Report event carries vendor bits
0x10/0x20/0x80 in Event_Type. Reports that should be ADV_DIRECT_IND
(0x01) arrive as 0x11/0x21/0x81/0x91/0xa1 (and occasionally 0x80/0x90/
0xa0/0x14/0x24 for the other types). process_adv_report() rejects them:

  Bluetooth: hci0: unknown advertising packet type: 0x81

Since directed advertising is exactly what bonded LE HID peripherals use
to reconnect, the passive-scan auto-connect never fires and a Logitech
MX Master 3 / Keychron K15 Max only reconnect when a report happens to
come through clean (observed ratio roughly 1 clean : 20 mangled). The
low nibble always holds a valid type (0x0-0x4), so strip the noise for
Broadcom controllers before validation.

Scope: keyed on the local controller's manufacturer, not on the peer,
and only for types that were being discarded anyway; reports with a
valid type (0x0-0x4) are untouched, so devices that work today see no
change. Low nibbles >= 5 remain invalid and are still dropped. The
BR/EDR path is not involved. Broadcom controllers with proper firmware
never emit such types, so the branch is inert there.

Verified with btmon on 7.0.10: with the fix the directed report is
parsed, LE Create Connection is issued immediately and the mouse is
encrypted and usable ~70 ms after waking; no "unknown advertising
packet type" messages remain.

Signed-off-by: Jose Miguel Ochoa <josemiguelo.ochoa@gmail.com>
---
 net/bluetooth/hci_event.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c b/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
index 1b3b913..10afa21 100644
--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_event.c
@@ -6424,6 +6424,16 @@ static void hci_le_adv_report_evt(struct hci_dev *hdev, void *data,
 
 		hci_store_wake_reason(hdev, &info->bdaddr, info->bdaddr_type);
 
+		/* Broadcom ROM firmware (e.g. BCM4364B3 on Apple T2 Macs
+		 * without a patchram) sets vendor bits 0x10/0x20/0x80 in the
+		 * legacy advertising Event_Type, so ADV_DIRECT_IND arrives as
+		 * 0x81/0x91/0xa1/... and every report is dropped as unknown:
+		 * bonded LE peripherals then never auto-reconnect. The real
+		 * type is in the low nibble; strip the noise.
+		 */
+		if (hdev->manufacturer == 15 && info->type > LE_ADV_SCAN_RSP)
+			info->type &= 0x0f;
+
 		if (info->length <= max_adv_len(hdev)) {
 			rssi = info->data[info->length];
 			process_adv_report(hdev, info->type, &info->bdaddr,
-- 
2.55.0

